# Readme conventions

When writing a readme document, it is most important to keep the file concise, i.e. short, well structured and understandable. 

Avoid using emojis. Prefer bullet points and script snippets over long text blocks.

- Before making any changes to the readme, make sure that the information you want to add is up to date and correct.
- After changing the readme, review it and make sure it
  - is well structured
  - contains every information exactly once (deduplicate)
  - is readable for humans

The Readme should start with a one paragraph text summary of the projects purpose.

This short summary should be followed by a very high level overview of what to find where in the repository. 

Then a usage section should explain 
- url(s) to access deployed stage(s)
- how to get a local dev setup running


Depending on the project, more information about important config/setup details and links where to find more information may follow.

The Readme should always end with a License section which states a copyright by European EPC COmpetence Center GmbH with a time range which should never be shortened but should be extended to include the current year, if it does not do so already. If the project is published under a specific License (OSS) the link to the license file and the necessary excerpt may also go into this section.


